<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#madhab">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madhab</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as madhhab</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A madhab is a school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-jurisprudence">Islamic jurisprudence</a>, a tradition of legal interpretation based on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> and Sunna. The four main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> schools, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a>, Shafii and Hanbali, took shape during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>. The Abbasid state drew on jurists from these schools in its legal administration.</p></div>
